// jhonstart/server — the request as a record, and the server-component
// convention.
//
// Promoted from `server.d.bp` (front 28 of 1.0.10-beta). The declaration file
// listed three blockers and all three are answered rather than carried:
//
//   - "the async SSR data layer … is gated on the effect-await surface" is
//     CLOSED. `#[@future] fn … -> @Future<T>` with a statement-level `await`
//     compiles and RUNS on both rows, `test` blocks included — measured here,
//     not assumed, and every `#[@future]` assertion in `test/server_test.bp`
//     executes on the erlang row.
//   - "`request()` is host-bound" stays true, and the host half ships WITH the
//     module, cell for cell on both rows, exactly as front 26's router does.
//   - "`Http` is a phantom `@Context` base (no members)" is DROPPED, along with
//     the `Request` behavior. `request()` is a hook anchored at `ElementBase`
//     (decisions 102/104 of 1.0.10-beta), so one base serves the whole render
//     tree; a second, memberless base would be a base nothing implements. `AGENTS.md` § *The `Http` base, and why it is gone*
//     carries the reasoning.
//
// ── Two things about this file that are NOT the front's README ──────────────
//
// 1. The six cells carry BOTH targets and name `jhonstart_server`, not
//    `rakun_request_context`.
//
//    The README binds them to front 62's erlang module. That is not writable,
//    and the reason is a measurement in each direction:
//
//      - An erlang-only cell reds the **commonJS compile** at its call site the
//        moment `request()` calls it — `` `__jhReqMethod` has no
//        `#[@External.<Target>(…)]` for the node backend `` — and this module is
//        compiled on both rows of the core member, so the whole member (51
//        landed assertions) stops compiling. Front 26 measured the same thing
//        for the router's five reads and carries the same note.
//      - `rakun_request_context` has no BEAM existence to bind to. rakun's
//        member is `targets: ["commonJS"]`, so on the erlang row — this front's
//        assigned target — every cell would answer `{error,undef}` at runtime
//        and no assertion that reads the request could RUN. Measured 2026-09-21
//        against compiler `2e6bb4ac`.
//
//    So the seam is HERE and it is `pub`, which is the conclusion front 26
//    already reached for the route snapshot and wrote down: a server that
//    reached into another library's process dictionary keys would be coupled to
//    them forever. `fillRequest` is the one writer; front 62's dispatcher calls
//    it once per request with the six strings it already has. If front 62 wants
//    its own module name to be the cell target instead, that is one line per
//    accessor in this file and nothing else in jhonstart moves — which is the
//    property the README was after.
//
// 2. The two querystring-encoded values are decoded with front 26's
//    `decodePairs`, not `std/querystring.parse`.
//
//    `libs/std/src/querystring.bp` is dead on the erlang row: reached through
//    `from "std"` it emits a call to a bare local `slice/3` it never defines,
//    `erlc` refuses the module and the first `parse` call dies `{error,undef}`.
//    Re-measured 2026-09-21 against compiler `2e6bb4ac`;
//    `repro/erlang-std-slice-shim/` is the jhonstart-free package. front 26
//    spelled the decoder in `router.bp` for exactly this reason and this file
//    imports it rather than growing a third copy — the same argument
//    `pairValue` makes about decoders.
//
// ── `@Future` is EAGER on erlang, and that changes how a page is written ─────
//
// `libs/std/src/http.bp:16-18` states it: "Erlang is eager: `@Future<T>`
// resolves to `T` … so the caller's `await fetch(url)` is identity on that
// backend." Two `#[@future]` loaders do NOT run in parallel because they are
// futures — they run in the order the body reaches them, and the page costs the
// SUM of its loaders.
//
// So a page with INDEPENDENT loaders does not await them one after another and
// hope. Parallel loading on the BEAM is a spawned process per unit of work,
// gathered by index, it takes **unstarted tasks** (`Array<fn() -> @Future<T>>`,
// i.e. `[{ -> loadPost(slug) }, { -> loadSidebar() }]`) rather than futures that
// have already run, and it is **front 02's**. This file ships no `awaitAll`,
// no `race` and no `allSettled`, and `docs.md` § *Server components* says who
// owns them.
//
// ── Where an `await` may stand ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// At statement level in a `#[@future]` body, and nowhere else. Never as the
// last statement of a lambda: `§2.38` makes a lambda's last statement an
// implicit-return expression and no file in this tree awaits inside one. A
// component that needs N rows awaits ONE loader returning `Array<T>` and maps
// synchronously afterwards — not N awaits inside a `map`.
//
// ── Escaping is not this file's ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `renderToString` writes `e.value` and every attribute value straight into the
// output (`element.bp:56`, `:63-65`) and neither escapes. A server component
// renders attacker-influenced text, so every such value goes through front 01's
// `escape.html` / `escape.attribute` at the point it enters the tree. This file
// hand-rolls no escaping and re-exports none: front 01's `libs/std/src/escape.bp`
// does not exist in compiler `2e6bb4ac` yet, and a stand-in here would be a
// second answer to "what is an escaped `&`" the day it lands.

import {Element, ElementBase, renderToString} from "element";
import {pairValue, decodePairs} from "router";

// ── step 1: the request as a record ─────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Six fields, every plural one an `Array<#(string, string)>` — the shape front
// 26's snapshot uses, the shape `decodePairs` produces and the shape
// `Element.attrs` takes, so a value read off the request is handed straight to
// an attribute with no conversion. No `Dict`: naming `dict.Dict<string, string>`
// as a type across a module boundary is unexercised anywhere in this tree, and
// the pair list is what actually crosses the wire.
//
// There is no `body` field, and its absence is a decision rather than an
// omission: a render never reads one. Form bodies are front 24's and
// route-handler bodies are front 25's.
//
// Field names and method names are kept DISJOINT, the same rule `RouterState`
// follows — `params` the field, `param(name)` the method, `query` the field,
// `queryParam(name)` the method — so a field read never shadows a method.
pub type RequestData(
    method: string,
    path: string,
    params: Array<#(string, string)>,
    query: Array<#(string, string)>,
    headers: Array<#(string, string)>,
    cookies: Array<#(string, string)>,
) {
    // Every accessor answers a plain `string`, `""` when absent, and never
    // raises. That is the ecosystem's decided shape and not laziness: rakun's
    // `Request` does exactly this (`repository/rakun/src/http.bp:30-34`). An
    // optional would force `.unwrapOr` at every call site in every page, and
    // `?T` handling is where the old drafts of this front went wrong.
    pub fn param(self: Self, name: string) -> string {
        return pairValue(self.params, name);
    }

    pub fn queryParam(self: Self, name: string) -> string {
        return pairValue(self.query, name);
    }

    pub fn header(self: Self, name: string) -> string {
        return pairValue(self.headers, name);
    }

    pub fn cookie(self: Self, name: string) -> string {
        return pairValue(self.cookies, name);
    }
}

// ── step 2: the host cells and the request builder ──────────────────────────
//
// Six reads and one write, dual-target for the reason at the top of this file.
// `src/sidecars/jhonstart_server.erl` and `src/server_runtime.mjs` are the two
// halves, cell for cell, so `test/server_test.bp` is ONE set of assertions run
// on both rows.
//
// The four pair-shaped values travel querystring-encoded (`k=v&k=v`), the same
// encoding front 26's `m`/`q` use and the same encoding front 23's payload
// carries. No JSON, no record serialization, nothing that has to agree between
// an Erlang term and a JS object.

#[@External.Node("./server_runtime.mjs", "method"),
  @External.Erlang("jhonstart_server", "method")]
declare fn __jhReqMethod() -> string;

#[@External.Node("./server_runtime.mjs", "path"),
  @External.Erlang("jhonstart_server", "path")]
declare fn __jhReqPath() -> string;

#[@External.Node("./server_runtime.mjs", "params"),
  @External.Erlang("jhonstart_server", "params")]
declare fn __jhReqParams() -> string;

#[@External.Node("./server_runtime.mjs", "query"),
  @External.Erlang("jhonstart_server", "query")]
declare fn __jhReqQuery() -> string;

#[@External.Node("./server_runtime.mjs", "headers"),
  @External.Erlang("jhonstart_server", "headers")]
declare fn __jhReqHeaders() -> string;

#[@External.Node("./server_runtime.mjs", "cookies"),
  @External.Erlang("jhonstart_server", "cookies")]
declare fn __jhReqCookies() -> string;

// The one writer, and the seam front 62's dispatcher calls once per request.
//
// Six values in TOGETHER, never one at a time, for the reason front 26's `fill`
// gives about the route: a half-updated request is a component reading the
// previous reader's cookie against this reader's path.
//
// Note that this is NOT front 26's `fill` under another name and the two stores
// stay separate on purpose. A route snapshot is re-filled DURING a render — its
// `selected` is the layout depth and changes per layout — while a request is
// filled once and is constant for the whole render. Folding them together would
// make "the request" mutate mid-page.
//
// The `-> i32` is the ecosystem's shape for a host cell whose value is not used
// (`router.bp`'s `__jhRouteFill`, `rakun/src/runtime.bp`'s `rkScan`). It is not
// a status code and callers ignore it.
#[@External.Node("./server_runtime.mjs", "fill"),
  @External.Erlang("jhonstart_server", "fill")]
declare fn __jhReqFill(
    method: string,
    path: string,
    params: string,
    query: string,
    headers: string,
    cookies: string,
) -> i32;

// Install the request for the render that follows. `params`, `query`, `headers`
// and `cookies` arrive querystring-encoded.
//
// Named `fillRequest` and not `fill`: front 26's `router.fill` is already `pub`
// and both modules are re-exported through the package root, so a consumer's
// `import { fill } from "jhonstart"` would have two candidates.
pub fn fillRequest(
    method: string,
    path: string,
    params: string,
    query: string,
    headers: string,
    cookies: string,
) -> i32 {
    return __jhReqFill(method, path, params, query, headers, cookies);
}

// The request the render reads. No `?T` unwrap that can fail: `decodePairs`
// answers `[]` for an empty string and every cell answers a total value, so an
// unfilled request is the empty request rather than a raise — which is what
// makes a component renderable in a test with no host present.
//
// A hook (decisions 102/104 of 1.0.10-beta): `val r = use request()` in a
// `#[@use]` server component — `#[@use] fn … -> @Component<Element>`, which may
// also `await` because `@Component` extends `@Future`. Called without `use` it
// answers the same `@Use` (a future on commonJS), so a `#[@future]` body or a
// test `await`s it.
#[@use]
pub fn request() -> @Use<ElementBase, RequestData> {
    return RequestData(
        method: __jhReqMethod(),
        path: __jhReqPath(),
        params: decodePairs(__jhReqParams()),
        query: decodePairs(__jhReqQuery()),
        headers: decodePairs(__jhReqHeaders()),
        cookies: decodePairs(__jhReqCookies()),
    );
}

// The only two re-exported shortcuts, because they are the two Next.js names a
// page reaches for without wanting the whole request. `after`, `connection`,
// `draftMode` and per-request memoization are front 62's and are called from
// there directly — re-declaring them here would be a second place for request
// scope to be defined.
pub fn cookies() -> Array<#(string, string)> {
    return decodePairs(__jhReqCookies());
}

pub fn headers() -> Array<#(string, string)> {
    return decodePairs(__jhReqHeaders());
}

// ── step 3: the server-component convention ─────────────────────────────────
//
// A server component is a `#[@future] pub fn` taking its route params and
// returning `@Future<Element>`. There is no decorator for it and there will not
// be one: the marker is `#[@future]`, which the language already ENFORCES in
// both directions —
//
//     pub fn f() -> @Future<i32>     →  a function returning `@Future`/`@Iterator`/
//                                       `@FutureGenerator` needs an effect annotation
//     #[@future] pub fn f() -> i32   →  effect-wrapper-mismatch: `#[@future]`
//                                       requires a `-> @Future<…>` return type
//
// — and a second marker would be a second thing to get wrong. A server
// component that also activates a hook is `#[@use] fn … -> @Component<Element>`
// (decision 104 of 1.0.10-beta: `@Future` grants no `use`, and `@Component`
// extends `@Future`, so `#[@use]` grants `use` and `await` both);
// `renderComponent` renders its thunk. R5 still makes `#[@future] #[@use]`
// `effect-duplicate-annotation`.
//
// The parameter is `fn() -> @Future<Element>` — an UNSTARTED thunk, not an
// already-running `@Future<Element>`. That is the same shape front 02's
// spawn-and-gather takes, so a page that grows a second loader moves from
// `renderServerComponent({ -> Page(params) })` to front 02 without changing what
// it hands anybody.
//
// **Call it with a lambda, never with a bare function name.** `{ -> Page(ps) }`
// is green on both rows; `renderServerComponent(Page)` compiles on commonJS and
// fails `variable 'Page' is unbound` on erlang — a compiler defect measured
// against `2e6bb4ac` and reported, not worked around. It is the same rule
// rakun's front 23 records for the fields of its `ElementView`.
#[@future]
pub fn renderServerComponent(component: fn() -> @Future<Element>) -> @Future<string> {
    val tree = await component();
    return renderToString(tree);
}

// A server component that activates a hook is `#[@use] fn … ->
// @Component<Element>` (decision 104 of botopink 1.0.10-beta: only `#[@use]`
// grants `use`, and `@Component` extends `@Future`, so the same body may
// `await` too). Its thunk is rendered here, as `renderServerComponent`
// renders a `#[@future]` one.
#[@future]
pub fn renderComponent(component: fn() -> @Component<Element>) -> @Future<string> {
    val tree = await component();
    return renderToString(tree);
}
